<h1>huge sparse array</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
Let N be the number of items to store and R be the size of the <a href="range.html"><em>range</em></a> of <a href="key.html"><em>key</em></a> values; R &gt;&gt; N.   Allocate, but don't initialize, two arrays: an item array I, where |I|&ge;N, and a location array L, where |L|=R. Initialize a variable, next, the number of items, to zero (with <a href="zeroBasedIndexing.html"><em>0-based indexing</em></a>). To insert an item, put it in the next place in the item array and save where to find it in the location array. <pre>   I[next] = item;<br />   L[item.key] = next;<br />   next++; </pre> To look up an item by key, get the index from the location array.  If the index is invalid or refers to the wrong item, the item is not found. <pre>   index = L[key];<br />   if (index &lt; 0 OR index &gt;= next) return NOTFOUND;<br />   if (I[index].key != key) return NOTFOUND;<br />   return I[index]; </pre> Inserting N items takes <a href="theta.html"><em>&Theta;(N)</em></a> total time, assuming allocation takes constant time. Retrieving an item by key (or responding "not found") takes constant time. Listing all items takes &Theta;(N) time using I.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="array.html"><em>array</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="sparsematrix.html"><em>sparse matrix</em></a>, <a href="hashtab.html"><em>hash table</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Keys must be unique.  Duplicate keys could be handled with <a href="collisionres.html"><em>collision resolution schemes</em></a>.   This data structure is usually impractical since the range is enormous. </p> 
<p> This effectively allocates a huge array without explicitly initializing it, at the cost of &Theta;(N) space and a little time for each access. </p> 
<p> This is what <a href="hashtab.html"><em>hash tables</em></a> want to be: constant time for insertion and look up.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Historical Note</strong><br>
Motivating problem first posed as exercise 2.12 in <strong>Alfred V. Aho,  John E. Hopcroft</strong>, and <strong>Jeffrey D. Ullman</strong>, <em>The Design and Analysis of Computer Algorithms</em>, page 71, Addison-Wesley, 1974.
</p>

